  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry weather, slightly below average \ntemperatures, and lower humidity to end the week and start the \nweekend. \n\nNorthwest flow continues both aloft and at the surface this \nafternoon. Sfc high pressure is still building in from the NW as the \nvertically stacked low to the NE slowly slides away. Drier air has \ndefinitely made it into the FA with dewpoints in the mid to upper \n50s for much of the area. The lower humidity combined with mostly \nsunny skies and temps in the mid 80s have all added up to a pretty \nnice late July day. Tomorrow will be pretty similar to today, though \nmaybe a couple degrees warmer. Saturday will also overall be pretty \nnice, but high pressure sliding offshore and the return of southerly \nflow means the humidity will start to creep back up. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A typical summertime pattern returns by late \nthis weekend and early next week with increasing shower/storm \nchances starting Sunday and continuing through early next week.\n\nThe pattern becomes more unsettled late in the weekend into early \nnext week as a slow moving UL trough approaches from the west. At \nthe same time, a weak cold front approaches from the north, but will \nremain north of the local area through Monday (and likely wash out \nby Tue). With the approaching trough and Bermuda high pressure well \noffshore, deep-layered S to SW flow will prevail. This will allow \nrich Gulf moisture to overspread the area on Sunday and remain over \nus early next week. PWs surge from a comfortable 1-1.4\" on Saturday \nto 2.2-2.4\" by late Sunday/Monday. Scattered to potentially numerous \nshowers/tstms are expected Sunday (with the highest chances inland). \nThen, showers/storms are likely across the entire area on Monday. \nThe highest storm coverage will be during the afternoon and evening \neach day. Given the high PWs, weak flow aloft, and tall, skinny \nCAPE...not really looking at much in the way of severe wx but there \nwill be a threat of localized flash flooding each day. The unsettled \npattern continues through at least Tuesday with the potential for \nadditional rounds of storms through the end of the week. Temps dip \ndown a little below average during the early week period thanks to \nthe cloud cover and multiple rounds of rain.",
